    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/f7940e73-d185-f70a-7aa1-6ada0dd09543/en": {
      "title": "LA  FACULTÉ  DES  ARTS   SOLLICITE  DES  DEMANDES  D’EMPLOI  À  TEMPS  PARTIEL   École  L’École  des  arts  libéraux  Titre  du  cours",
      "description": "     LA  FACULTÉ  DES  ARTS   SOLLICITE  DES  DEMANDES  D’EMPLOI  À  TEMPS  PARTIEL   École  L’École  des  arts  libéraux  Titre  du  cours  \nLe\n \nfrançais\n \net\n \nles\n \nmédias\n  Code  et  numéro  du  cours  \nLANG\n \n3016\n \nFL12\n Description  du  cours  Consulter  https://laurentian.ca/academics/courses  Date  d’entrée  en  fonction  mai  2027  Fin  du  contrat  juillet  2027  Nombre  de  séances  10  Horaire  du  cours  Asynchrone  Période  des  examens  le  23-24  juillet  Inscriptions  prévues  20  Nombre  de  crédits  3.00  Méthode  de  prestation  LU  en  ligne  (asynchrone)   Diplôme  requis  M.A.  est  souhaité,  PhD  préférable  Rémunération  Total  du  traitement  comprenant  le  traitement  de  base  ainsi  que  les  montants,  tenant  lieu  de  vacances  et  d’avantages  sociaux  conformément  à  la  Convention  collective  \n Conformément  à  l’article  4.15.14,  il  faut  faire  parvenir  une  lettre  de  candidature ,  un  curriculum  \nvitae\n \nà\n \njour\n,\n \net\n \ntoute\n \ndocumentation\n \npertinente\n \nappuyant\n \nla\n \ncandidature\n \nà :\n  Directrice  :  Christine  Sansalone  Unité :  \nL’École  des  arts  libéraux  \nUniversité  Laurentienne,  935  chemin  du  lac  Ramsey,  Sudbury,  Ontario,  P3E  2C6  Courriels:  sla@laurentian.ca   La  date  de  clôture  est  le :  le  30  octobre  2026  \n \n Il  appartient  au  Doyen  de  nommer  les  professeurs  au  nom  du  vice-recteur  aux  études. Tous  les  postes  seront  \ncomblés\n \nsous\n \nréserve\n \nd’approbation\n \nbudgétaire.\n \nL’Université\n \nse\n \nréserve\n \nle\n \ndroit\n \nd’annuler\n \nce\n \ncours\n \nsi\n \nle\n \nnombre\n \nd’inscriptions\n \nest\n \ninsuffisant.\n \nD’autres\n \npostes\n \npourraient\n \nêtre\n \ndisponibles.\n \nVeuillez\n \ncontacter\n \nle\n \ndirecteur\n \nou\n \nla\n \ndirectrice\n \ndu\n \ndépartement\n \npour\n \ntout\n \nrenseignement\n \nsupplémentaire.\n \n  \nÀ\n \ncompter\n \ndu\n \n1\n er\n mai\n \n2022,\n \nla Politique  et  le  programme  sur  la  vaccination  contre  la  COVID-19 sont  mis  en  suspens.  Cela  signifie  que  cette  politique  et  ce  programme  ne  s’appliquent  pas  pour  le  moment,  mais  ils  ne  \nsont\n \npas\n \nannulés\n \net\n \npourraient\n \nêtre\n \nrétablis\n \nsi\n \nles\n \nconditions\n \nde\n \nla\n \nsanté\n \npublique\n \nle\n \njustifient,\n \nou\n \nselon\n \nles\n \ndirectives\n \ndes\n \ninstances\n \nfédérales,\n \nprovinciales\n \nou\n \nlocales\n \nde\n \nsanté  L’Université  Laurentienne  est  une  communauté  englobante  et  accueillante  et  encourage  les  candidatures  de  \nmembres\n \nde\n \ncommunautés\n \nqui\n \nrecherchent\n \nl’équité,\n \ny\n \ncompris\n \nles\n \nfemmes\n \net\n \nles\n \npersonnes\n \nracialisées,\n \nautochtones,\n \nhandicapées\n \net\n \nde\n \ntoutes\n \norientations\n \nsexuelles\n \net\n \nidentités\n \net\n \nexpressions\n \nsexuelles.\n Les  membres  du  corps  professoral  de  l’UL  font  partie  de  l’APPUL  (l’Association  des  professeures  et  professeurs  de  l’Université  Laurentienne).  La  Convention  collective  est  disponible  au  site  www.lufappul.ca.     \n"
    },
    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/616b8590-0e9a-98b8-db3f-6ad643dee683/en": {
      "title": "Tenure Track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in Pharmaceutical Chemistry",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position: Pharmaceutical Chemist with Application in \nNeuropharmacology \n \nThe School of Natural Sciences in the Faculty of Science, Engineering and Architecture at \nLaurentian University invites applications from suitably qualified candidates for an existing \nTenure Track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in Pharmaceutical Chemistry \nwith Application in Neuropharmacology. The preferred start date is July 1, 2027. \n \nCandidates must have completed a PhD degree in a relevant field, such as pharmaceutical \nchemistry, neuropharmacology, molecular neuroscience, or a related discipline, at the start of \nthe appointment. Relevant work experience at a post-secondary institution is an asset. \nCandidates with proven research expertise in drug design, development and synthesis, drug-\nreceptor interactions and neuropharmacological mechanisms of drug action are encouraged to \napply. The ideal candidate will teach courses in our Pharmaceutical Chemistry and Behavioural \nNeuroscience B.Sc. programs, and will contribute to French-language lecture instruction. The \ncandidate will build a strong research program, secure external funding, and supervise students \nat the undergraduate and graduate levels. It is expected that the successful candidate will forge \ninterdisciplinary research interactions and collaborations with colleagues in the School of \nNatural Sciences, with other units at the university, and with industry and community partners. \n \nThe successful candidate will have the following essential qualifications: \n• A doctoral degree in chemistry, pharmacology, or neuroscience/neuropharmacology at \nthe time of appointment. \n• Active knowledge of both official languages (functional bilingualism), and will teach \ncourses in French and in English; consideration of receptive bilingualism (passive \ncomprehension of the French/English language) will be given in exceptional cases. \n• A demonstrated commitment to pedagogical and academic excellence; teaching \nexperience relevant to one or more of pharmacology, neurochemistry, organic \nchemistry, molecular modeling, medicinal chemistry, drug design, development, or \nsynthesis is an asset. \n• A track record of publications in peer-reviewed literature on topics related to \npharmacology, neuropharmacology, medicinal chemistry, drug design, development, \nand synthesis. \n• Commitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and work \nenvironment. \n \nIn addition to active engagement and participation in teaching and research, the successful \ncandidate will be expected to have an on-campus presence and make contributions through \nservice to the School, Faculty, University, and broader community. \n \nThe School of Natural Sciences is the newest and largest academic unit in the Faculty of Science, \nEngineering & Architecture. Our faculty members are actively involved in research projects \ncovering a wide range of fields, including chemistry, environmental science, biomedical science, \nforensic science, materials science, neurobiology, zoology as well as science communication. A \nvariety of specialized research equipment is currently available on campus in the Perdue \nCentral Analytical Facility (PCAF) and a new Virtual Reality Lab is available for advanced \nunderstanding of molecular interactions.  \n \nLaurentian University, located on Atikameksheng Anishnawbek territory in Sudbury, Ontario, \nserves over 8000 students and is one of two bilingual universities in the province of Ontario. \nCommitted to its bilingual, tricultural mandate, Laurentian University offers an outstanding \nuniversity experience in English and French with a comprehensive approach to Indigenous \neducation. Laurentian’s beautiful, forested campus is surrounded by fresh-water lakes, \nconservation lands and hundreds of kilometres of cross-country and hiking trails while situated \nin the geographic centre of Greater Sudbury, northern Ontario’s major urban centre \n(population 160,000). Laurentian University has close and productive ties to Science North, \nSNOLAB, Health Sciences North and multiple post-secondary institutions. \n \nApplication Requirements \nApplications should include the following items: \n• A cover letter which clearly indicates the area(s) of expertise. The letter must also \ninclude one of the two statements about citizenship/resident status specified below. \n•  Complete and up-to-date Curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications. \n• A teaching statement, which would contain a teaching philosophy, strategies for student \nengagement, etc. \n• A research statement, including a description of potential research projects the \ncandidate would initiate at Laurentian. \n• A statement providing evidence of incorporation of Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) \ninto both teaching and research. \n• Names and full contact information for three academic referees. \n \nThe application package, inclusive of all documents, should be sent electronically as a single \nPDF file to: Office of Provost and Vice-president Academic at academic-careers@laurentian.ca \nwith the following subject line: Application for faculty position in Pharmaceutical Chemistry. \nReview of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 but applications will be accepted until \nthe position is filled. Further information about the position should be directed to the Office of \nProvost and Vice-president Academic. \n \nLaurentian University is an inclusive and welcoming community committed to employment \nequity. Applications are encouraged from members of equity-seeking communities including \nwomen, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual \norientations and gender identities/expressions. Laurentian University's bilingualism policy \n(Section 7.3.b) provides a provision regarding the language requirement for persons self-\nidentifying as First Nations, Métis or Inuit. (https://laurentian.ca/bilingualism) The successful \ncandidate will be part of the Laurentian University Faculty Association (LUFA). Candidates are \nencouraged to consult the Collective Agreement at www.lufappul.ca. The minimum salary for \nthe rank of Assistant Professor is set at $ 89, 914.52, with additional increments awarded for \nrelevant experience in accordance with the Laurentian University Faculty Association Collective \nAgreement. \n \nLaurentian University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to \napplicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage \nduring the recruitment process. Please contact the Office of the Provost and Vice-President \nAcademic for more information (pvpa@laurentian.ca). \n \nAll qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian \nimmigration requirements, Canadian citizens and permanent residents of Canada will be given \npriority. To comply with federal laws, the University is obliged to gather statistical information \nas to how many applicants for each job vacancy are Canadian citizens / permanent residents of \nCanada. Applicants need not identify their country of origin or citizenship; however, all \napplicants must include one of the following statements: “I am a Canadian citizen / permanent \nresident of Canada”; OR, “I am not a Canadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada” in their \ncover letter. Applications that do not include this information \nwill be deemed incomplete. \n "
    },
    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/64e66832-d8ce-428e-83b8-6a79e0c78bbb/en": {
      "title": "digital fabrication, computational design, wood technologies and design-build. Teaching",
      "description": " \nTenure Track Assistant Professor Position in Architecture – \nDigital Fabrication, Design Computation, and Design/Build \n \nThe McEwen School of Architecture (MSoA) in the Faculty of Science, Engineering and \nArchitecture at Laurentian University invites applications from suitably qualified candidates \nfor a faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. The preferred start date is July 1, \n2027. \n  \nThe candidates will have expertise in the following areas emphasizing making and craft: \ndigital fabrication, computational design, wood technologies and design-build. Teaching \nresponsibilities will include introductory courses in computational design, architectural \ncommunication, and digital fabrication, along with advanced courses in these fields. The \nsuccessful applicant should also be prepared to teach design studio and be able to offer \ninstruction in related areas, including technology, robotics, sustainability, and wood \nconstruction. \n  \nCandidates should demonstrate a collaborative approach and a strong interest in \ncontributing to the school’s newly established Institute for Northern Housing Innovation \n(INHI). They should also be prepared to engage with related disciplines across the \nuniversity, including engineering, computer science, and other relevant fields. \n  \nAll candidates must have a professional degree in architecture. Academic post-graduate \nspecialized degrees, including a doctorate, are preferred. Practicing architects should \ndemonstrate that they are registered interns or licensed members of a professional \nassociation, along with a proven design record. Preference will be given to candidates who \nare fluent in written and spoken French and those who support the tri-cultural mandate of \nLaurentian University and the MSoA. The tri-cultural mandate is a commitment to English, \nFrench, and Indigenous cultures and perspectives in education. Candidates need to \ndemonstrate a commitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and \nwork environment. \n  \nThe successful candidate will have the following essential qualifications: \n• A professional degree in Architecture at the time of appointment. \n• A post-graduate degree, such as a doctorate is preferred; and/or \n• Registration or Licensure as a member of an Architecture Professional Association (with \nrelevant expertise in the area of Digital Fabrication, Computational Design and Design-\nBuild). \n• Have active knowledge of both official languages (functional bilingualism); consideration \nof receptive bilingualism (passive comprehension of the French/English language) will be \ngiven in exceptional cases. \n• Experience in teaching courses in architectural design, digital fabrication, computational \ndesign and design/build. The candidate will have demonstrated a commitment to \npedagogical and academic excellence. \n• Evidence of a creative research stream, including published work, that advances \nknowledge in Digital Fabrication, Computational Design, Wood Technology, and Design-\nBuild practices. \n• Experience and expertise in wood and timber technologies and systems would be \nconsidered an asset. \n• Commitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and work \nenvironment. \n  \nIn addition to active engagement and participation in teaching, the successful candidate is \nexpected to make contributions through service to the School, Faculty, University, and \nbroader community. An on-campus presence is also expected. \n \nEstablished in 2013, the McEwen School of Architecture (MSoA) offers a four-year pre-\nprofessional Bachelor of Architectural Studies (BAS) degree, followed by a two-year \nprofessional Master of Architecture degree (M.Arch), accredited by the Canadian \nArchitectural Certification Board. Situated in northern Ontario, the MSoA focuses on design \nwith wood, design/build, and community design projects. Our integrated co-operative \nprogram enables students to obtain practical experience through work-term placements in \nindustry and in design or architecture offices. Francophone, First Nations, Métis, and Inuit \nstudent experience is central to the unique tri-cultural mandate of the program. The \narchitecture program is delivered in English, with French language options for design \nstudios, elective courses, and co-operative placements. Through the tri-cultural mandate \nand bilingual options, students are exposed to a unique array of methods, knowledge and \ndirect experience that informs community-design and design-build exercises. \n  \nThe Institute for Northern Housing Innovation (INHI) is a newly established Senate-\nApproved Research Centre at Laurentian University focused on improving housing \naffordability and sustainability. The new 3,200 square foot mass timber addition is \nconceived as both a living lab and a pillar of sustainable construction, enabling assembly \ntesting, advanced manufacturing, and robotic fabrication research for students and \nindustry alike. It aims to establish a nationally recognized training centre dedicated to \nadvancing specialized education and strengthening industry and community partnerships. \n  \nThe MSoA seeks innovative individuals with established records of design, practice, and a \npassion for teaching. Individuals should be ready to teach in the classroom, the design \nstudio, fabrication labs, and in the field with local community groups and partnerships. The \nsuccessful candidate will teach in both the undergraduate and graduate architecture \nprograms and will participate in the administration and governance of the University, \nincluding service to MSoA committees and institution building. All faculty members are \nexpected to teach architectural design in addition to developing expertise in a particular \nstream of the curriculum: Design, Culture, Technology, or Professional Practice. Laurentian \nis a bilingual university with a tricultural mandate, as such, fluency in French for this \nposition is preferred. \n  \nLaurentian University, located on Atikameksheng Anishnawbek territory in Sudbury, \nOntario, serves over 8000 students and is one of two bilingual universities in the province \nof Ontario. Committed to its bilingual, tri cultural mandate, Laurentian University offers an \noutstanding university experience in English and French with a comprehensive approach to \nIndigenous education. Laurentian’s beautiful, forested campus is surrounded by fresh-\nwater lakes, conservation lands and hundreds of kilometres of cross-country and hiking \ntrails while situated in the geographic centre of Greater Sudbury, northern Ontario’s major \nurban centre (population 160,000). Laurentian University has close and productive ties to \nScience North, SNOLAB, Health Sciences North and multiple post-secondary institutions. \nApplication Requirements \nApplications should include the following items: \n• A cover letter which clearly indicates the area(s) of expertise. The letter must also \ninclude one of the two statements about citizenship/resident status specified below. \n• Complete and up-to-date Curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications. \n• A teaching statement, which would contain a teaching philosophy, strategies for student \nengagement, etc. \n• A portfolio of professional design works and/or design work by students taught by the \nApplicant. \n• A portfolio of creative research demonstrating expertise in digital fabrication, \ncomputational design and design/build. \n• A statement providing evidence of incorporation of Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) \ninto teaching. \n• Names and full contact information for three academic referees. \n \nThe application package, inclusive of all documents, should be sent electronically as a s ingle \nPDF file to: Office of Provost and Vice -president Academic at academic -\ncareers@laurentian.ca with the following subject line: Application for tenure -track faculty \nposition in Digital Fabrication, Computational Design and Design/Build. Review of \napplications will begin on January 1, 2027 but applications will be accepted until the position \nis filled. Further information about the position should be directed to the Office of Provost \nand Vice-president Academic. \n \nLaurentian University is an inclusive and welcoming community committed to employment \nequity. Applications are encouraged from members of equity-seeking communities including \nwomen, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual \norientations and gender identities/expressions. Laurentian University's bilingualism policy \n(Section 7.3.b) provides a provision regarding the language requirement for persons self-\nidentifying as First Nations, Métis or Inuit. (https://laurentian.ca/bilingualism) The successful \ncandidate will be part of the Laurentian University Faculty Association (LUFA). Candidates are \nencouraged to consult the Collective Agreement at www.lufappul.ca. The minimum salary for \nthe rank of Assistant Professor is set at $ 89, 914.52, with additional increments awarded for \nrelevant experience in accordance with the Laurentian University Faculty Association Collective \nAgreement. \n  \nLaurentian University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to \napplicants with accessibility needs. Requests for ac commodation can be made at any stage \nduring the recruitment process. Please contact the Office of the Provost and Vice -President \nAcademic for more information (pvpa@laurentian.ca). \n  \nAll qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian \nimmigration requirements, Canadian citizens and permanent residents of Canada will be \ngiven priority.  To comply with federal laws, the University is obliged to gather stati stical \ninformation as to how many applicants for each job vacancy are Canadian citizens / \npermanent residents of Canada. Applicants need not identify their country of origin or \ncitizenship; however, all applicants must include one of the following statements: “I am a \nCanadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada”; OR, “I am not a Canadian citizen \n/ permanent resident of Canada” in their cover letter. Applications that do not include this \ninformation will be deemed incomplete. \n  \n  \n "
    },
    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/6a89201e-4daa-9ee5-0df7-6ab172bfd9c3/en": {
      "title": "Master Lecturer Position in Chemical Engineering",
      "description": " \n \nMaster Lecturer Position in Chemical Engineering \n \nThe School of Engineering and Computer Science in the Faculty of Science, Engineering and Architecture \nat Laurentian University invites applications from suitably qualified candidates for a faculty position in \nChemical Engineering at the rank of Master Lecturer. The preferred start date is July 1, 2027.  \n \nApplicants will hold undergraduate and graduate degrees in Chemical Engineering (or closely related \ndisciplines) at the start date of appointment.  The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong \npotential for outstanding teaching contributions in Chemical Engineering, and an ongoing commitment \nto academic and pedagogical excellence in support of the school’s programs. Candidates from all areas \nof chemical engineering are encouraged to apply; however, expertise in metallurgy, applied \nthermochemistry, or environmental sustainability, would be viewed as an asset. \n \nAs a faculty member at Laurentian University, the successful applicant will also be a member of the \nLaurentian University Faculty Association (LUFA).  Detailed information about working conditions is \nprovided within the Collective Agreement between LUFA and Laurentian University, available at \nhttp://www.lufappul.ca.  In addition to being actively engaged in teaching, the successful candidate will \nbe expected to have an on-campus presence. \n \nThe successful candidate will have the following essential qualifications: \n \n● Hold at least a Master’s degree in Chemical Engineering or a closely related discipline.  Holding a \nDoctoral degree in Chemical Engineering or a closely related discipline is an asset. \n● Satisfaction of eligibility requirements for registration as a Professional Engineer in Canada. \n● Experience in teaching undergraduate and/or graduate level university courses in Chemical \nEngineering. \n● Demonstrated commitment to pedagogical and academic excellence. \n● Demonstrated commitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and work \nenvironment. \n● Ability to prioritize according to students’ learning needs, and possess a good rapport with \nstudents. \n● Excellent communication skills in English.  An active knowledge of both official languages \n(functional bilingualism) would be considered as an asset. \n \nLaurentian University, located on Atikameksheng Anishnawbek territory in Sudbury, Ontario, serves over \n8000 students and is one of two bilingual universities in the province of Ontario. Committed to its \nbilingual, tricultural mandate, Laurentian University offers an outstanding university experience in \nEnglish and French with a comprehensive approach to Indigenous education. Laurentian’s beautiful, \nforested campus is surrounded by fresh-water lakes, conservation lands and hundreds of kilometres of \ncross-country and hiking trails while situated in the geographic centre of Greater Sudbury, northern \nOntario’s major urban centre (population 160,000). Laurentian University has close and productive ties \nto Science North, SNOLAB, Health Sciences North and multiple post-secondary institutions. \n \n \n  \n\nApplication Requirements \nApplications should include the following items:  \n \n● A cover letter. The letter must also include one of the two statements about \ncitizenship/resident status specified below. \n● Complete and up-to-date Curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications. \n● A teaching statement, which would contain a teaching philosophy, strategies for student \nengagement, etc.  \n● A statement providing evidence of incorporation of Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) into \nteaching.  \n● Names and full contact information for three academic references  (only contacted following \nfinal interview). \n \nThe application package, inclusive of all documents, should be sent electronically as a single PDF file to: \nOffice of Provost and Vice-president Academic at academic-careers@laurentian.ca with the following \nsubject line: Application for master lecturer position in Chemical Engineering. Review of applications will \nbegin on December 1, 2026 but applications will be accepted until the position is filled.  \nFurther information about the position should be directed to the Office of Provost and Vice-president \nAcademic. \nLaurentian University is an inclusive and welcoming community committed to employment equity. \nApplications are encouraged from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized \nand Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender \nidentities/expressions. Laurentian University's bilingualism policy (Section 7.3.b) provides a provision \nregarding the language requirement for persons self-identifying as First Nations, Métis or Inuit. \n(https://laurentian.ca/bilingualism) The successful candidate will be part of the Laurentian University \nFaculty Association (LUFA). Candidates are encouraged to consult the Collective Agreement at \nwww.lufappul.ca. The minimum salary for the rank of Master Lecturer is set at $74,587.36, with \nadditional increments awarded for relevant experience in accordance with the Laurentian University \nFaculty Association Collective Agreement. \nLaurentian University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants \nwith accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment \nprocess. Please contact the Office of the Provost and Vice-President Academic for more information \n(pvpa@laurentian.ca).   \nAll qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration \nrequirements, Canadian citizens and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. \nTo comply with federal laws, the University is obliged to gather statistical information as to how many \napplicants for each job vacancy are Canadian citizens / permanent residents of Canada. Applicants need \nnot identify their country of origin or citizenship; however, all applicants must include one of the \nfollowing statements: “I am a Canadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada”; OR, “I am not a \nCanadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada” in their cover letter. Applications that do not include \nthis information will be deemed incomplete. "
    },
    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/774648bf-ab4d-aee1-0abe-6adf6e8aae3a/en": {
      "title": "A Master Lecturer is a full-time faculty teaching-focused position, with some course release",
      "description": " \nMaster Lecturer Position in Architecture – Environmental Sustainability and \nPerformance, Building Science, and Building Systems \n \nThe McEwen School of Architecture in the Faculty of Science, Engineering and Architecture \nat Laurentian University invites applications from suitably qualified candidates for a faculty \nposition at the rank of Master Lecturer. The preferred start date is July 1, 2027. \n \nA Master Lecturer is a full-time faculty teaching-focused position, with some course release \nfor research or governance. The maximum teaching workload per academic year is twenty-\nfour (24) credits of which 3 credits may be assigned for research or governance.  Each \ntrimester studio course is 6 credits, other courses are 3 credits.   In addition to active \nengagement and participation in teaching, the successful candidate may contribute \nthrough service to the School, Faculty, University, and broader community. An on-campus \npresence is expected. Master Lecturers, full-time faculty members and as such, they have \nan equal voice in University and School governance, and have the same opportunities to \nteach undergraduate or graduate courses, including thesis supervision, development of \ncurricula, etc. Salary and benefits for Master Lecturers at Laurentian University are \ncommensurate with Professors.  Master Lecturers are eligible for permanency (equivalent \nto tenure for Professors) after three years of full-time employment, and they may apply for \nmerit salary increases every three years. In addition, Master Lecturers are eligible for a paid \nsix-month professional development leave after six consecutive years of full-time service in \nthe rank of Master Lecturer. \n \nApplicants must have demonstrable expertise in architectural systems design and in \nteaching. Applicants must share how sustainability impacts their design and teaching or \npractice. In addition, successful candidates will have expertise in one or more of the \nfollowing areas: Environmental Sustainability and Performance, Building Science, and \nBuilding Systems. The successful candidate may potentially teach Professional Practice and \nco-op courses. \n \nPreference will be given to applicants with a minimum of 10 years of practice and/or \nteaching experience. A commitment to quality teaching (including professional \nmentorship) is essential. \n \nAll applicants must have a professional degree in architecture. Applicants with a \nprofessional degree in a directly related field may be considered if they have demonstrable \nexperience teaching architectural design studio.  Academic post-graduate specialized \ndegrees are an asset.  Practicing architects should demonstrate that they are registered \ninterns or licensed members of a professional association, along with a proven design \nrecord. Fluency in written and spoken French will be considered an asset. Applicants \nshould demonstrate support for the tri-cultural mandate of Laurentian and a commitment \nto promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and work environment. The tri-\n\ncultural mandate is a commitment to English, French, and Indigenous cultures and \nperspectives in education. \n \nThe successful candidate will have the following essential qualifications: \n• A professional degree in Architecture. Applicants with a professional degree in a directly \nrelated field may be considered if they have demonstrable experience teaching \narchitecture studio. \n• A post- professional degree in a related area of expertise is an asset. \n• Practicing architects should demonstrate that they are registered interns or licensed \nmembers of a professional association. \n• Experience in teaching courses in architectural design and at least one of the areas of \nspecialization: Environmental Sustainability and Performance, Building Science, and \nBuilding Systems. The successful candidate will have demonstrated a commitment to \npedagogical and academic excellence in design, this may also include professional \nmentorship. \n• A track record of design works related to one of the areas of specialization: \nEnvironmental Sustainability and Performance, Building Science, and Building Systems. \n• Commitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and work \nenvironment. \n \nThe MSoA seeks innovative individuals with established records of design, practice, and a \npassion for teaching. Individuals should be ready to teach in the classroom, the design \nstudio, fabrication labs, and in the field with local community groups and partnerships. The \nsuccessful candidate will teach in both the undergraduate and graduate architecture \nprograms and may participate in the administration and governance of the University, \nincluding service to MSoA committees and institution building. All faculty members are \nexpected to teach architectural design in addition to developing expertise in a particular \nstream of the curriculum: Design, Culture, Technology, or Professional Practice. Laurentian \nis a bilingual university with a tricultural mandate, as such, fluency in French for this \nposition is an asset. \n \nEstablished in 2013, the McEwen School of Architecture (MSoA) is an award-winning state-\nof-the-art facility located in downtown Greater Sudbury, Ontario, Canada. The School offers \na four-year pre-professional Bachelor of Architectural Studies (BAS) degree, followed by a \ntwo-year professional Master of Architecture degree (M.Arch), accredited by the Canadian \nArchitectural Certification Board. Situated in northern Ontario, the MSoA focuses on design \nwith wood, design/build, and community design projects. Our integrated co-operative \nprogram enables students to obtain practical experience through work-term placements in \nindustry and in design or architecture offices. Francophone, First Nations, Métis, and Inuit \nstudent experience is central to the unique tri-cultural mandate of the program. The \narchitecture program is delivered in English, with French language options for design \nstudios, elective courses, and co-operative placements. Through the tri-cultural mandate \nand bilingual options, students are exposed to a unique array of methods, knowledge and \ndirect experience that informs community-design and design-build exercises. \n \nThe Institute for Northern Housing Innovation (INHI) is a newly established Senate-\nApproved Research Centre at Laurentian University focused on improving housing \naffordability and sustainability. The new 3,200 square foot mass timber addition to the \nMSoA’s downtown campus is conceived as both a living lab and a pillar of sustainable \nconstruction, enabling assembly testing, advanced manufacturing, and robotic fabrication \nresearch for students, faculty and industry alike. It aims to establish a nationally recognized \ntraining centre dedicated to advancing specialized education and strengthening industry \nand community partnerships. \n \nLaurentian University, located on Atikameksheng Anishnawbek territory in Sudbury, \nOntario, serves over 8000 students and is one of two bilingual universities in the province \nof Ontario. Committed to its bilingual, tri-cultural mandate, Laurentian University offers an \noutstanding university experience in English and French with a comprehensive approach to \nIndigenous education. Laurentian’s beautiful, forested campus is surrounded by fresh-\nwater lakes, conservation lands and hundreds of kilometres of cross-country and hiking \ntrails while situated in the geographic centre of Greater Sudbury, northern Ontario’s major \nurban centre (population 160,000). Laurentian University has close and productive ties to \nScience North, SNOLAB, Health Sciences North and multiple post-secondary institutions. \n \nApplication Requirements \nApplications should include the following items: \n \n• A cover letter which clearly indicates experience in the area(s) of expertise. The letter \nmust also include one of the two statements about citizenship/resident status specified \nbelow. \n• Complete and up-to-date Curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications. \n• A teaching statement, which would contain a teaching philosophy, strategies for student \nengagement, etc. \n• A portfolio of professional design works and/or design work by students taught by the \nApplicant. \n• A statement providing evidence of incorporation of Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) into \nteaching. \n• Names and full contact information for three professional or academic referees. \n \nThe application package, inclusive of all documents, should be sent electronically as a single \nPDF file to: Office of Provost and Vice-president Academic at academic-\ncareers@laurentian.ca with the following subject line: Application for Master Lecturer \nposition in Architecture: Environmental Sustainability and Performance, Building Science, \nand Building Systems. Review of applications will begin on January 1, 2027 but applications \nwill be accepted until the position is filled. \n \nFurther information about the position should be directed to the Office of Provost and Vice-\npresident Academic. \n \nLaurentian University is an inclusive and welcoming community committed to employment \nequity. Applications are encouraged from members of equity-seeking communities including \nwomen, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all \nsexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. Laurentian University's bilingualism \npolicy (Section 7.3.b) provides a provision regarding the language requirement for persons \nself-identifying as First Nations, Métis or Inuit. (https://laurentian.ca/bilingualism)The \nsuccessful candidate will be part of the Laurentian University Faculty Association (LUFA). \nApplicants are encouraged to consult the Collective Agreement at www.lufappul.ca. The \nminimum salary for a Master Lecturer is set at $74,587.36, with additional increments \nawarded for relevant experience in accordance with the Laurentian University Faculty \nAssociation Collective Agreement. \n \nLaurentian University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to \napplicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage \nduring the recruitment process. Please contact the Office of the Provost and Vice-President \nAcademic for more information (pvpa@laurentian.ca). \n \nAll qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian \nimmigration requirements, Canadian citizens and permanent residents of Canada will be \ngiven priority.  To comply with federal laws, the University is obliged to gather statistical \ninformation as to how many applicants for each job vacancy are Canadian citizens / \npermanent residents of Canada. Applicants need not identify their country of origin or \ncitizenship; however, all applicants must include one of the following statements: “I am a \nCanadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada”; OR, “I am not a Canadian citizen/ \npermanent resident of Canada” in their cover letter. Applications that do not include this \ninformation will be deemed incomplete. \n \n \n "
    },
    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/8416f631-40e0-06a5-a027-6ad6410f43eb/en": {
      "title": "Master Lecturer Position – Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": " \nMaster Lecturer Position – Mechanical Engineering \n \nThe School of Engineering and Computer Science in the Faculty of Science, Engineering and Architecture \nat Laurentian University invites applications from suitably qualified candidates for a faculty position in \nMechanical Engineering at the rank of Master Lecturer. The preferred start date is July 1, 2027*.  \n \nApplicants will hold undergraduate and graduate degrees in M echanical Engineering (or closely related \ndisciplines, e.g., Mechatronics Engineering)  at the start date of appointment.  The successful candidate \nwill demonstrate a strong potential for outstandin g teaching contributions in Mechanical Engineering at \nthe undergraduate level, and an ongoing commitment to academic and pedagogical excellence in support \nof the school’s programs. \n \nAs a faculty member at Laurentian University, the successful applicant will also be a member of the \nLaurentian University Faculty Association (LUFA).  Detailed i nformation about working conditions is  \nprovided within the Collective Agreement between LUFA and Laurentian University, available at \nhttp://www.lufappul.ca.  In addition to being actively engaged in teaching, the successful candidate will \nbe expected to have an on-campus presence. \n \nThe successful candidate will have the following essential qualifications: \n \n● Hold at least a Master’s degree in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline.  Holding \na Doctoral degree in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline is an asset. \n● Satisfaction of eligibility requirements for registration as a Professional Engineer in Canada. \n● Experience in teaching undergraduate  and/or graduate level university  courses in Mechanical \nEngineering. \n● Demonstrated commitment to pedagogical and academic excellence. \n● Demonstrated c ommitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and  work \nenvironment. \n● Ability to prioritize according to students’ learning needs, and posse ss a good rapport with \nstudents. \n● Excellent communication skills in English.  An active knowledge of both official languages \n(functional bilingualism) would be considered as an asset. \n \nLaurentian University, located on Atikameksheng Anishnawbek territory in Sudbury, Ontario, serves over \n8000 students and is one of two bilingual universities in the province of Ontario.  Committed to its \nbilingual, tricultural mandate, Laurentian University offers an outstanding university experience in English \nand French with a comprehensive approach to Indigenous education. Laurentian’s beautiful, forested \ncampus is surrounded by fresh -water lakes, conservation lands and hundreds of kilometres of cross -\ncountry and hiking trails while situated in the geographic centre of Greater Sudbury, northern Ontario’s \nmajor urban centre (population 160,000). Laurentian University has close and productive ties to Science \nNorth, SNOLAB, Health Sciences North and multiple post-secondary institutions. \n \n  \nApplication Requirements \nApplications should include the following items:  \n \n• A cover letter . The letter must also include one of the two statements about \ncitizenship/resident status specified below. \n• Complete and up-to-date Curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications. \n• A teaching statement, which would contain a teaching philosophy, strategies for student \nengagement, etc.  \n• A statement providing evidence of incorporation of Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) into \nteaching.  \n• Names and full contact information for three references. \n \nThe application package, inclusive of all documents, should be sent electronically as a single PDF file to: \nOffice of Provost and Vice -president Academic at academic-careers@laurentian.ca with the following \nsubject line: Application for faculty position in Mechanical Engineering. Review of applications will begin \non January 22, 2027 but applications will be accepted until the position is filled.  \nFurther information about the position should be directed to  the Office of Provost and Vice -president \nAcademic. \nLaurentian University is an inclusive and welcoming community committed to employment equity. \nApplications are encouraged from members of equity -seeking communities including women, racialized \nand Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender \nidentities/expressions. Laurentian University's bilingualism policy (Section 7.3.b) provides a provision \nregarding the language requirement for persons self -identifying as First Nations, Métis or Inuit. \n(https://laurentian.ca/bilingualism) The successful candidate will be part of the Laurentian University \nFaculty Association (LUFA). Candidates are encouraged to consult the Collective Agreement at \nwww.lufappul.ca. The minimum salary for the rank of Master Lecturer is set at $74,587.36, with additional \nincrements awarded for relevant experience in accordance with the Laurentian University Faculty \nAssociation Collective Agreement. \nLaurentian University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with \naccessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. \nPlease contact the Office of the Provost  and Vice -President Academic for more information \n(pvpa@laurentian.ca).   \nAll qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration \nrequirements, Canadian citizens and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority.   \nTo comply with federal laws, the University is obliged to gather statistical information as to how many \napplicants for each job vacancy are Canadian citizens / permanent residents of Canada. Applicants need \nnot identify their country of origin or citizenship; however, all applicants must include one of the following \nstatements: “I am a Canadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada”; OR, “I am not a Canadian citizen / \npermanent resident of Canada” in their cover letter. Applications that do not include this information will \nbe deemed incomplete.  \n* Pending approval of the University's 2027-28 budget by the Board of Governors.  "
    },
    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/ae8b6d04-83ce-c4c9-f690-6ab1716fa642/en": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Position in Computer Science",
      "description": " \nTenure Track Position in Computer Science \n \nThe School of Engineering and Computer Science in the Faculty of Science, Engineering and \nArchitecture at Laurentian University invites applications from suitably qualified candidates for a \ntenure-track faculty position in Computer Science, at the rank of Assistant/Associate Professor. The \nsuccessful candidate will have expertise in Cyber Security, Artificial Intelligence, or closely related \nareas. Expertise in one or more of the following areas is considered an asset: Cloud Computing, Data \nScience, Machine Learning, Generative AI, Natural Language Processing, Software Engineering, Full \nStack Software Development, Internet of Things (IoT), Distributed Systems, or Quantum Computing. \nThe preferred start date is July 1, 2027.  \n \nApplicants must have completed a PhD degree by the start date of the appointment. In addition to \nactive engagement and participation in teaching and research, the successful candidate will be \nexpected to make contributions through service to the School, Faculty, University, and broader \ncommunity.   \n \nAs a faculty member at Laurentian University, the successful applicant will also be a member of the \nLaurentian University Faculty Association (LUFA).  Detailed information about working conditions is \nprovided within the Collective Agreement between LUFA and Laurentian University, available at \nhttp://www.lufappul.ca. \n \nThe successful candidate will have the following essential qualifications: \n \n● A doctoral degree in Computer Science or a closely related field. \n● Evidence of good research and scholarship potential. \n● Experience in teaching at both the undergraduate and/or graduate levels coupled \n● Evidence of the ability to teach one or more of the following topics would be an asset: \no Undergraduate level: object-oriented programming (e.g., Java, C++, Python), \nLinux/UNIX systems, data structures and algorithms, computer networks, operating \nsystems, database systems, software engineering, computer architecture and assembly \nlanguage, and cyber security.  \no Graduate level: Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning and Deep Learning, Generative \nAI and Large Language Models, Cyber Security, Cloud Computing, Data Science, Natural \nLanguage Processing, Internet of Things (IoT), Quantum Computing, and Advanced \nSoftware Engineering.  \n● Demonstrated commitment to pedagogical and academic excellence. \n● Demonstrated commitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive, and vibrant academic and work \nenvironment. \n● Commitment to promoting a diverse, inclusive and vibrant academic and work environment. \n● Excellent communication skills in English.  An active knowledge of both official languages \n(functional bilingualism) would be considered an asset. \n \nLaurentian University, located on Atikameksheng Anishnawbek territory in Sudbury, Ontario, serves \nover 8000 students and is one of two bilingual universities in the province of Ontario. Committed to its \nbilingual, tri-cultural mandate, Laurentian University offers an outstanding university experience in \nEnglish and French with a comprehensive approach to Indigenous education. Laurentian’s beautiful, \n\nforested campus is surrounded by freshwater lakes, conservation lands and hundreds of kilometres of \ncross-country and hiking trails while situated in the geographic centre of Greater Sudbury, northern \nOntario’s major urban centre (population 160,000). Laurentian University has close and productive ties \nto Science North, SNOLAB, Health Sciences North and multiple post-secondary institutions.  \n \nApplication Requirements \nA complete application includes the following:  \n● A cover letter. The letter must also include one of the two statements about \ncitizenship/resident status specified below. \n● An up-to-date Curriculum Vitae. \n● A statement of current and prospective research interests \n● A teaching statement, which would contain a teaching philosophy, strategies for student \nengagement, etc.  \n● A statement providing evidence of commitment to principles of Equity, Diversity and Inclusion \n(EDI) \n● The names and contact information for three academic references (only contacted following \nfinal interview) \n \nThe application package, inclusive of all documents, should be sent electronically as a single PDF file to: \nOffice of the Provost and Vice -President Academic at academic-careers@laurentian.ca with the \nfollowing subject line: Application for faculty position in Computer Science. Review of applications will \nbegin on December 1, 2026 but applications will be accepted until the position is filled.  \nLaurentian University is an inclusive and welcoming community committed to employme nt equity. \nApplications are encouraged from members of equity -seeking communities including women, racialized \nand Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender \nidentities/expressions. Laurentian University 's bilingualism policy (Section 7.3.b) provides a provision \nregarding the language requirement for persons self -identifying as First Nations, Métis or Inuit. \n(https://laurentian.ca/bilingualism) The successful candidate will be part of the Laurentian Unive rsity \nFaculty Association (LUFA). Candidates are encouraged to consult the Collective Agreement at \nwww.lufappul.ca. The minimum salary for the rank of Assistant Professor is set at $ 89, 914.52, with \nadditional increments awarded for relevant experience in accordance with the Laurentian University \nFaculty Association Collective Agreement. The minimum salary for the rank of Associate Professor is set at \n$ 107,819.26, with additional increments awarded for relevant  experience in accordance with the \nLaurentian University Faculty Association Collective Agreement. \n \nLaurentian University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier-free experience to applicants with \naccessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment \nprocess. Please  contact the Office of the Provost and Vice -President Academic for more information \n(pvpa@laurentian.ca). \n \nAll qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration \nrequirements, Canadian citizens and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. \n \nTo comply with federal laws, the University is obliged to gather statistical information as to how many \napplicants for each job vacancy are Canadian citizens / permanent residents of Canada. Applicants need \nnot identify their country of origin or citizenship; however, all applicants must include one of the following \nstatements: “I am a Canadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada”; OR, “I am not a Canadian citizen / \npermanent resident of Canada” in their cover letter. Applications that do not include this information will \nbe deemed incomplete.  \n \n "
    },
    "https://laurentian.ca/about/careers/faculty-vacancies/eb208986-1f11-b0cb-d4d0-6ad63b1dbddb/en": {
      "title": "POSTING FOR A TENURE-TRACK FACULTY POSITION IN INDIGENOUS STUDIES (ANISHINAABEMOWIN)",
      "description": "  \n  \n \n \n \nPOSTING FOR A TENURE-TRACK FACULTY POSITION IN INDIGENOUS STUDIES \n(ANISHINAABEMOWIN) \n  \nThe School of Liberal Arts  in the  Faculty of Arts  at Laurentian University invites applications from \nsuitably qualified candidates for a full -time position in Anishinaabemowin at a rank matching \nqualifications and experience. Depending on the candidate's qualifications, expertise, and career focus, \nthe appointment may be made as either a Master Lecturer (focusing on pedagogical excellence, \ncurriculum development, and student mentorship) or a tenured/tenure -track faculty member \n(Lecturer, Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor focusing on scholarly inquiry, grant acquisition, and \nresearch-informed teaching). Candidates are requested to specify their chosen stream in their cover \nletter. The preferred start date is July 1, 2027, and the position will remain open until filled.  \n  \nThe successful candidate will play a pivotal role in shaping Anishinaabemowin curriculum, teaching \nstudents at various levels of proficiency, and engaging in community-driven research or language \nrevitalization initiatives in Indigenous Studies within the School of Liberal Arts. The ideal candidate will \nhave the following qualifications:  \n  \n● Indigenous ( First Nations, Inuit, and M étis) to Turtle Island with demonstrated ties to their \ncommunity.  \n● Comprehensive understanding of Indigenous worldviews and frameworks.  \n● Understanding of Indigenous ( First Nations, M étis and Inuit) to Turtle  Island culture and \ntraditions including their importance within an Indigenous Studies framework.  \n● A Ph.D. in Indigenous Studies, Indigenous Education, Linguistics, or a closely related field is \npreferred. Candidates who possess a master’s degree alongside  extensive, recognized lived \nexperience as a community language expert will be given full and equal consideration.   \n● Applicants of all educational backgrounds who bring extensive, recognized lived experience in \nIndigenous language fluency and teaching —including Elders, Knowledge Keepers, and \ncommunity language experts — are welcome and will be considered. As part of the selection \nprocess, candidates may undergo an oral or practical language proficiency assessment \nconducted by a fluent speaker or community language expert. \n● Experience designing, developing, and delivering courses in Anishinaabemowin , ranging from \nintroductory to advanced fluency levels with preference given to undergraduate and graduate \ncourses. \n● Evidence of the ability to teach effectively at the undergraduate level with a commitment to \npedagogical and academic excellence, promoting a safe, inclusive, and welcoming learning \nenvironment, and advancing Laurentian University’s tricultural mandate.  \n● As applicable, demonstrated research/scholarly/creative productivity and teaching experience \nin their area(s) of expertise. \n● Commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion, promoting a vibrant academic and work \nenvironment.  \n  \n\nActively engaging in teaching and/or research respective of the type of appointment, the successful \ncandidate will establish a strong presence on campus, contributing to the School, Faculty, University, \nand broader community. The candidate will teach in -person as well as advance student learning in \ncurriculum design and program oversight, all within a collegial environment.  \n  \nLaurentian University’s School of Liberal Arts provides a dynamic tricultural and bilingual community. \nOur research and our teaching in matters of language, culture, thought, creativity, and the law \ncontribute to transformative understandings within the region, across the country, and around the \nworld. Liberal Arts faculty are integral to the Faculty’s graduate programs in Relational Studies (M.A.) \nas well as Human Studies and Interdisciplinarity (Ph.D.) along wi th undergraduate degree programs in \nFrench as a Second Language; English Literature, Media, and Writing; History, Interdisciplinary Studies; \nand Law & Justice.  \n  \nLaurentian University, located on Atikameksheng Anishnawbek territory in Sudbury, Ontario, se rves \nover 8000 students and is one of two bilingual universities in the province of Ontario. Committed to its \nbilingual, tri -cultural mandate, Laurentian University offers an outstanding university experience in \nEnglish and French with a comprehensive appr oach to Indigenous education. Laurentian’s beautiful, \nforested campus is surrounded by fresh- water lakes, conservation lands, and hundreds of kilometres \nof cross-country and hiking trails while situated in the geographic centre of Greater Sudbury, northern \nOntario’s major urban centre (population 160,000). Laurentian University has close and productive ties \nto Science North, SNOLAB, Health Sciences North and multiple post-secondary institutions.   \nApplication Requirements  \nA complete application includes the following:   \n● A cover letter (including one of the two statements about citizenship/resident status specified \nbelow)  \n● An up-to-date Curriculum Vitae.    \n● A statement of current and prospective teaching experience and interests   \n● A statement of current and prospective research interests (as applicable)   \n● Self-identification as First Nations, Métis or Inuit*   \n● The names and contact information for three academic references.  \n  \nThe application package, inclusive of all documents, should be submitted electronically as single PDF \nfile to:   \n  \nOffice of Provost and Vice -President Academic at academic -careers@laurentian.ca with the following \nsubject line: applicati on for faculty position in Indigenous Interdisciplinary Studies . The deadline for \nreceipt of applications is December 15, 2026, or until the position is filled.   \n \n * First Nations, Métis and Inuit Verification will be required for applicants who advance to the interview \nprocess (certificate of Indian Status, federally recognized Métis organization in Canada, and/or \nverification from an Inuit organization/community); or letter of explanation if one cannot be provided. \nApplicants will also be required to submit a letter of belonging from their community.  \n \nLaurentian University is an inclusive and welcoming community committed to employment equity. \nApplications are encouraged from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized \nand Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender \nidentities/expressions. Laurentian University’s bilingualism policy (Section 7.3.b) provides a provision \nregarding the language requirement for persons self -identifying as First Nations, Métis, or Inuit. \n(https://laurentian.ca/bilingualism) The successful candidate will be part of the Laurentian University \nFaculty Association (LUFA). Candidates are encouraged to consult the Collective Agreement at \nwww.lufappul.ca.The salary range i s $74,587.36 to $132,382.60, depending on the academic rank \n(Lecturer, Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Full Professor) awarded at the time of hire, in \naccordance with the Laurentian University Faculty Association Collective Agreement. \n \nLaurentian University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier -free experience to applicants \nwith accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment \nprocess. Please contact the Office of the Provost and Vice -President Academic for more information \n(pvpa@laurentian.ca).    \nAll qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration \nrequirements, Canadian citizens and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority.    \nTo comply with federal laws, the University is obliged to gather statistical information as to how many \napplicants for each job vacancy are Canadian citizens / permanent residents of Canada. Applicants need \nnot identify their country of origin or citizenship; however, all applicants must include one of the \nfollowing statements: “I am a Canadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada”; OR, “I am not a \nCanadian citizen / permanent resident of Canada” in their cover letter. Applications that do not include \nthis information will be deemed incomplete.    \n  "
    }
  },
